WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.800
SPEAKER: When it
comes to security,

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:03.870
Google Cloud is never finished.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.700
There's always room
for improvement,

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.590
and our newest
security improvement

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.480
is providing keyless
authentication

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.710
for service accounts.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:13.840
When your application
needs to access services

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.510
in Google Cloud outside
of the scope of a user,

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:19.060
it probably uses
a service account.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:20.530
Service accounts
allow developers

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:22.630
to assign an identity
to an application

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:25.610
and then give that identity
specific permissions,

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:28.870
for example, to access
a cloud storage bucket.

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:31.900
Service account keys are
akin to user passwords,

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.260
allowing the holder to act as
the service account and gain

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:39.190
access to any resource that
service account has access to.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:41.200
Unfortunately, there's
no way to verify

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.290
that the application holding the
key has permission to use it.

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:46.390
It's a key without
an expiration date

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:48.730
and with no guarantee
around where it's stored

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:51.100
or who has access to it.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.190
Because of this risk, managing
the storage, distribution,

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:55.960
and rotation of
service account keys

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:58.300
becomes a top
priority, effectively

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.060
turning an identity management
problem into a secrets

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:02.660
management problem.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.569
So what's the solution?

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:06.190
Ditch the keys.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:08.080
Workload identity
federation enables

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.450
applications running
outside of Google Cloud

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:12.280
to replace long-lived
service account

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.360
keys with temporary
credentials, also called tokens.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.120
Workload identity federation
is a keyless application

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.630
authentication mechanism for
calling Google Cloud APIs.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:25.360
It works by first having
your application authenticate

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.690
to your identity provider and
receive account credentials.

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:31.330
The application can then call
our security token service

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:33.580
to exchange the account
credentials issued

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:37.090
by your identity provider for a
short-lived Google Cloud access

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:38.500
token.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.290
This access token can then be
used to impersonate a service

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:43.510
account, inheriting the
permissions of that service

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:46.270
account to access
Google Cloud resources.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:48.340
To set up workload
identity federation,

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:50.770
you'll first need to create
a workload identity pool

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:52.540
in your Google Cloud project.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.413
Fortunately, you don't need to
be a super admin to do this.

00:01:55.413 --> 00:01:57.580
You only need permission
to manage workload identity

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:01.840
pools, service accounts, and IAM
policies at the project level.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.840
A workload identity pool allows
you to organize and manage

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.010
external identities.

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:08.800
A project can have multiple
pools with each one

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.860
allowing access from a different
external identity provider.

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:14.110
This allows you to create
collections of identities

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:16.270
and easily control the
permissions granted

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:19.380
to identities from
each identity provider.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.650
Then, configure a one-way trust
between your identity provider

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.680
and the workload identity pool
by providing relevant metadata

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:28.050
about your provider.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.780
Now, when an application
attempts to exchange their IDP

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.752
credential, the
security token service

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:34.710
will be able to validate
that the credential is

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.470
from a trusted provider
before issuing an access

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.170
token to the application.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.620
Finally, you'll need to
create an IAM policy that

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.810
allows identities in the
workload identity pool

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:46.850
to impersonate the
service account.

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:48.530
With these policies
in place, we're

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:51.380
now set up to properly
authenticate the application.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:54.230
We've mitigated a security risk
while maintaining the ability

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:56.990
to reliably make Google
Cloud API requests.

00:02:56.990 --> 00:02:57.680
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.110
To learn more about how
workload identity federation can

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:02.180
help you better protect
your Google Cloud access,

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:03.650
check out the blog post.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:06.410
To try it out for yourself, be
sure to see the documentation

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:08.780
to start your keyless
security adventure.

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:12.130
[MUSIC PLAYING]

